```JavaScript
{
    var Infinity = 1;
    console.log(Infinity); // Infinity
}
{
    let Infinity = 1;
    console.log(Infinity); // 1
}
```

For the top-level cases, it is necessary to consider different scenarios. 
When it comes to browsers, the result can be affected by whether it is an ESM or not. (or strict mode)

```html
<script>
console.log(Infinity); //  SyntaxError: Identifier 'Infinity' has already been declared
function Infinity() {}
</script>
```

```html
<script type="module">
console.log(Infinity); // ƒ Infinity() { }
function Infinity() { }
</script>
```

For Node.js, whether it's ESM or not, `Infinity` is always a shadowed reference.

```JavaScript
console.log(Infinity); // [Function: Infinity]
function Infinity() { }
```
